window.AIBRIEFS=window.AIBRIEFS||{};Object.assign(window.AIBRIEFS,{"1988/87-5259":{"facts":"Teague, a black man, was tried in an Illinois court for attempted murder, armed robbery and aggravated battery. The prosecutor used all 10 of his peremptory challenges to strike black venire members, producing an all-white jury. Defense counsel twice moved for a mistrial, saying Teague was entitled to a jury of his peers, and the prosecutor said he was seeking a balance of men and women. The trial court denied the motions and Teague was convicted.","procedure":"The Illinois Appellate Court rejected Teague's fair cross section claim, and review in Illinois and the Supreme Court was denied. In a federal habeas petition he repeated that claim and argued that opinions in McCray v. New York undermined Swain v. Alabama. The district court denied relief; the en banc Seventh Circuit held he could not benefit from Batson v. Kentucky, that his Swain claim was procedurally barred, and that the fair cross section requirement covers only the venire.","question":"May a state prisoner on federal habeas corpus obtain the benefit of Batson, press an equal protection claim under Swain that he never raised in state court, or invoke a new rule extending the Sixth Amendment fair cross section requirement to the petit jury, and under what standard are new rules of criminal procedure applied on collateral review?","holding":"Allen v. Hardy bars use of Batson because Teague's conviction became final before Batson, and his Swain claim is procedurally barred under Wainwright v. Sykes. A plurality adopted Justice Harlan's approach: new constitutional rules of criminal procedure generally do not apply to cases on collateral review unless they fall within one of two narrow exceptions, and neither applies to the petit-jury fair cross section rule, so the Court would not announce it.","reasoning":"On the first two points, the Court said a denial of certiorari carries no precedential weight, so the McCray opinions did not destroy Swain, and Teague forfeited the Swain claim by not raising it at trial or on direct appeal and showed no cause for the default. Justice O'Connor's plurality treated retroactivity as a threshold question because a rule applied to the defendant in the case announcing it must be applied evenhandedly to similarly situated defendants. The older Linkletter standard had produced inconsistent and unequal results, while Griffith v. Kentucky had already adopted Harlan's rule for cases on direct review. Habeas corpus is a collateral remedy, not a substitute for appeal, and interests of comity and finality, along with the burden on States of retrying defendants whose trials met the standards then in force, weigh against applying new rules to final convictions. Harlan's exceptions are rules placing private conduct beyond the power to criminalize and watershed rules, which the plurality limited to procedures without which the likelihood of an accurate conviction is seriously diminished. A petit-jury cross section rule is not such a “bedrock procedural element.” The plurality added that habeas “cannot be used as a vehicle to create new constitutional rules of criminal procedure” that would not be applied retroactively.","separate":"Justice White concurred in the judgment and in Parts I to III: he still disagreed with the direct-review retroactivity cases but accepted the result as consistent with them. Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment as to the Swain claim and joined Part I of Justice Stevens's opinion. Justice Stevens, joined in Part I by Justice Blackmun, concurred in part and in the judgment: he thought the Sixth Amendment claim valid and the Court should decide it, objected to treating retroactivity as a threshold question and to narrowing the fundamental fairness exception to accuracy, but felt bound by Allen v. Hardy, and he would have treated the Swain claim as unexhausted. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented: the plurality adopted a sweeping new limit on habeas without briefing or argument and contrary to precedent and stare decisis, it would bar many constitutional claims, and Teague's claim was a modest extension of Taylor, Duren and Batson.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was affirmed. Justice O'Connor announced the judgment and wrote the opinion of the Court as to Parts I, II and III, and for a plurality (with the Chief Justice and Justices Scalia and Kennedy) as to Parts IV and V; Justices Brennan and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep489/usrep489288/usrep489288.pdf"},"1983/82-472":{"facts":"Russello owned a building in Tampa and arranged for arsonists, members of an arson ring, to burn part of it so he could collect insurance. The fire spread, and he received four payments from the insurer totaling $340,043.09, helped by a ring member who served as adjuster and was paid $30,000 out of the proceeds.","procedure":"Russello was convicted in federal district court of RICO racketeering and conspiracy under 18 U.S.C. section 1962(c) and (d), and the jury returned special verdicts forfeiting the insurance payments under section 1963(a)(1). A Fifth Circuit panel reversed the forfeiture, but the en banc court affirmed it by a vote of 16 to 7, in conflict with the Ninth Circuit's Marubeni decision, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do the profits and proceeds of racketeering count as an “interest” acquired in violation of section 1962 that is subject to forfeiture under section 1963(a)(1), or does that provision reach only interests in an enterprise?","holding":"Profits and proceeds of racketeering are an “interest” within section 1963(a)(1) and are subject to forfeiture. The provision is not limited to interests in an enterprise.","reasoning":"Justice Blackmun began with the statutory text. Because RICO does not define “interest,” the Court assumed its ordinary meaning, which covers all forms of real and personal property, including profits and proceeds, and every property interest can be called an interest in something. Congress chose a broad term consistent with RICO's pattern of using general terms like enterprise and racketeering activity. The structure of the statute confirms this: subsection (a)(2) expressly limits forfeiture to an interest in an enterprise, and the omission of that limit from (a)(1) is presumed intentional, especially since an early bill's limiting phrase was dropped from the first subsection. The two subsections are not redundant, since (a)(1) reaches illegally acquired interests whether or not in an enterprise while (a)(2) reaches enterprise interests even if lawfully acquired. A narrow reading would blunt the provision against illegitimate enterprises, which rarely hold assets because proceeds are quickly distributed. The specific mention of profits in the Controlled Substances Act, subsequent bills, and state statutes did not limit the broad term. The legislative history shows Congress meant to attack organized crime's economic base, which depends on its profits, and directed that the statute be liberally construed. The rule of lenity does not apply because the language is clear, and “the rule of lenity does not come into play.”","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was affirmed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep464/usrep464016/usrep464016.pdf"},"1979/79-521":{"facts":"The Consumer Product Safety Commission obtained accident reports from manufacturers under the Consumer Product Safety Act (CPSA), most of them accompanied by claims of confidentiality. After the Consumers Union and Public Citizen's Health Research Group filed Freedom of Information Act (FOIA) requests, the Commission decided to release the reports, including those claimed to be confidential, without following section 6(b)(1) of the CPSA. That section requires 30 days' notice to a manufacturer whose identity the public could readily ascertain, a chance to comment, and reasonable steps to ensure the information is accurate and its disclosure fair.","procedure":"Manufacturers sued to block release. The District Court granted summary judgment and permanently enjoined disclosure, holding that section 6(b)(1) applies to FOIA releases and falls within FOIA Exemption 3. The Third Circuit affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict with the Second Circuit.","question":"Does section 6(b)(1) of the Consumer Product Safety Act govern the Commission's disclosure of records in response to a Freedom of Information Act request?","holding":"Yes. Section 6(b)(1) applies to public disclosures made in response to FOIA requests as well as to disclosures the Commission initiates. The Commission's practical objections did not change that result.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statutory text, which covers the public disclosure of any information obtained under the Act, and FOIA requesters are part of the public. The exceptions in section 6(b)(2) do not include FOIA releases, although Congress knew of the FOIA and incorporated its exemptions in section 6(a)(1), and section 25(c) makes certain reports subject to section 6(b) whether released affirmatively or on request. The pre-enactment history showed industry and congressional concern about disclosure generally, and committee and administration comments drew no distinction between the two kinds of release. Later statements, including a remark by Representative Moss and a 1976 conference report, deserve little weight because they came from a later Congress or were isolated and unpersuasive, and the Commission's interpretation was recent and arguably litigation-driven. Section 6(b)(1) sets definite criteria, so it falls under FOIA Exemption 3, which defeats the claimed conflict with FOIA's time limits, and claims of administrative burden are for Congress.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Rehnquist delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447102/usrep447102.pdf"},"1981/80-846":{"facts":"Noah Lundy was convicted in a Tennessee court of rape and crime against nature and sentenced to long consecutive terms. After the state appellate courts affirmed and he lost a state postconviction petition, he sought federal habeas relief under 28 U.S.C. § 2254 on four grounds: a limit on cross-examination of the victim, a prosecutor's reference to his violent character, a prosecutor's closing remark that the State's evidence was uncontradicted, and a jury instruction that every witness is presumed to swear the truth. Only the first two had been presented to the state courts.","procedure":"The District Court said it could not consider the third and fourth claims because they were unexhausted, but it looked at them \"collaterally\" in assessing the trial's atmosphere, also reviewed other instances of prosecutorial misconduct never raised in state court, and granted the writ. The Sixth Circuit affirmed and rejected the State's argument that the petition should have been dismissed because it mixed exhausted and unexhausted claims. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the exhaustion requirement of 28 U.S.C. §§ 2254(b) and (c) require a federal district court to dismiss a habeas petition that contains both exhausted and unexhausted claims?","holding":"Yes. A district court must dismiss a \"mixed petition\" containing both exhausted and unexhausted claims, leaving the prisoner to return to state court to exhaust the rest or to amend or resubmit the petition with only exhausted claims.","reasoning":"The statute and its history do not address mixed petitions, so the Court looked to the purposes of the exhaustion doctrine, which rests on comity and on protecting the state courts' role in enforcing federal law. A total exhaustion rule gives state courts the first chance to review all federal claims, makes them more familiar with federal issues, and produces a fuller factual record for federal review. The District Court's own statement that the violations were mixed so that one could not be separated from the others showed how hard it is for courts to decide when claims are interrelated and how tempting it is to consider unexhausted ones. The Court viewed the rule as simple and clear for pro se prisoners and expected it to reduce piecemeal litigation. In a plurality part (O'Connor, joined by Burger, Powell and Rehnquist), the opinion added that the rule does not unduly delay relief because the prisoner may delete the unexhausted claims, though doing so risks dismissal of later petitions as an abuse of the writ under Habeas Rule 9(b).","separate":"Blackmun concurred in the judgment: he would have held that only the unexhausted claims of a mixed petition are dismissed while the district court rules on exhausted claims, argued that the Court's rule traps unwary pro se prisoners and adds delay, and would remand to dismiss the unexhausted claims and reconsider the rest. Brennan (joined by Marshall) joined the Court's opinion but not Part III-C, arguing that the Rule 9(b) abuse-of-the-writ issue was not before the Court and that a prisoner who drops unexhausted claims because the court refused a mixed petition cannot be said to have abused the writ. White concurred in part and dissented in part, agreeing with most of Brennan's opinion but, like Blackmun, preferring that the court rule on exhausted claims unless they are intertwined with the dismissed ones. Stevens dissented, arguing that the District Court properly judged the exhausted claims in the context of the whole trial, that district judges should have discretion over mixed petitions, and that the rule is needless procedural lawmaking that delays relief.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice O'Connor announced the judgment and wrote the opinion of the Court (Parts I, II, III-A, III-B and IV, joined by Burger, Brennan, Marshall, Powell and Rehnquist); Part III-C was a plurality opinion.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep455/usrep455509/usrep455509.pdf"},"1987/86-1461":{"facts":"A construction company allegedly paying substandard wages was building a department store for a tenant in a Tampa shopping mall owned by the DeBartolo Corporation. A union with a dispute against the contractor peacefully handed out leaflets at the mall's four entrances for about three weeks, asking customers not to shop at any mall store until the owner promised that mall construction would use contractors paying fair wages. The handbills said the union sought only a consumer boycott and did not ask anyone to stop work or deliveries, and there was no picketing or patrolling.","procedure":"On DeBartolo's charge under § 8(b)(4) of the National Labor Relations Act, the Board first dismissed the complaint under the statute's publicity proviso, but the Supreme Court held the proviso inapplicable and remanded. On remand the Board held the handbilling violated § 8(b)(4)(ii)(B) as coercion and declined to reach First Amendment issues. The Eleventh Circuit denied enforcement, construing the statute not to reach such handbilling to avoid serious constitutional doubts.","question":"Does § 8(b)(4)(ii)(B) of the NLRA, which bars unions from threatening, coercing, or restraining a neutral to cease doing business with another, prohibit peaceful handbilling urging a consumer boycott of a mall's stores, or should the statute be read to avoid the First Amendment question?","holding":"The statute can fairly be read not to reach peaceful handbilling, unaccompanied by picketing, urging a consumer boycott of neutral employers, and that reading avoids serious First Amendment questions. Neither the statutory language nor the legislative history clearly shows that Congress meant to prohibit such handbilling.","reasoning":"The Board's reading would ordinarily get deference, but the Court applied the rule that \"where an otherwise acceptable construction of a statute would raise serious constitutional problems, the Court will construe the statute to avoid such problems unless such construction is plainly contrary to the intent of Congress.\" The Board's reading raised serious First Amendment questions because the handbills truthfully disclosed a labor dispute and urged a lawful course of action. The words \"threaten, coerce, or restrain\" are vague and should be read with caution, and handbilling that relies on persuasion alone is different from picketing, which was the concern in Tree Fruits and Safeco. The publicity proviso can be read as a clarification of what is not coercive rather than an exception to a ban on all publicity, since the Board's reading would reach newspaper and radio appeals and even union meetings. The legislative history showed concern with secondary picketing, only opponents of the bill said it reached leaflets, and Senator Kennedy's remarks and the conference summary indicated that publicity short of picketing remained free.","separate":"Justices O'Connor and Scalia concurred in the judgment without a written opinion. Justice Kennedy took no part.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Rehnquist and Justices Brennan, Marshall, Blackmun and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep485/usrep485568/usrep485568.pdf"},"1980/79-1404":{"facts":"Pennsylvania owns and operates Pennhurst State School and Hospital, an institution housing about 1,200 mentally retarded residents. Terri Lee Halderman, a resident, sued on behalf of all residents in 1974, alleging that conditions at Pennhurst were unsanitary, inhumane and dangerous and seeking injunctive and monetary relief and the closing of Pennhurst in favor of community living arrangements. The United States and the Pennsylvania Association for Retarded Citizens intervened as plaintiffs. The District Court's findings about the conditions were undisputed, and Pennsylvania participated in the federal-state grant program created by the Developmentally Disabled Assistance and Bill of Rights Act, receiving about $1.6 million under it in 1976.","procedure":"After a 32-day trial the District Court certified a class and held that the residents had constitutional and statutory rights to minimally adequate habilitation in the least restrictive environment, ordering that Pennhurst eventually be closed. The en banc Third Circuit substantially affirmed, but avoided the constitutional claims and rested on 42 U.S.C. section 6010 of the Act, finding that it created substantive rights enforceable through an implied cause of action; it also relied on state law and did not require closing Pennhurst. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does section 6010, the “bill of rights” provision of the Developmentally Disabled Assistance and Bill of Rights Act, create in favor of the mentally retarded substantive rights to appropriate treatment in the least restrictive setting that bind participating States?","holding":"No. Section 6010 is a statement of congressional findings and policy that expresses a preference for certain kinds of treatment; it creates no substantive rights and does not condition federal funds on the States providing such treatment. The Court left open whether private parties may enforce the Act's specific conditions (sections 6011 and 6063(b)(5)(C)), and remanded the state-law, constitutional and Rehabilitation Act claims.","reasoning":"Congress's intent to legislate under section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment is not lightly inferred, especially where the asserted rights impose affirmative funding obligations; here the Act's language and structure show a funding statute under the spending power. Spending Clause legislation is much in the nature of a contract, so a condition on federal funds must be stated unambiguously so that States can accept knowingly. Section 6010 contains no conditional language, in contrast to sections 6005, 6009, 6011, 6012 and 6063, and the Court called it “too thin a reed to support the rights and obligations read into it by the court below.” The legislative history shows that the Conference Committee dropped the Senate's detailed standards and funding sanction, and the Secretary of HHS had not read section 6010 as a condition; the modest $1.6 million grant also pointed to a limited purpose. Comparison with the specific conditions in sections 6011, 6063(b)(5)(C) and 6062 showed that Congress knew how to impose duties and would have had no reason for them if section 6010 already imposed absolute obligations. The Court noted open questions about private enforcement, whether Pennhurst is a “program assisted”, remedy and the Eleventh Amendment, and remanded them.","separate":"Justice Blackmun concurred in part and in the judgment. He joined most of the opinion but not Part IV, objecting to its apparent negative attitude toward private enforcement of sections 6011 and 6063, and he thought section 6010 should be given some meaning through its link to section 6063 and that Pennhurst should not escape the Act because it receives no funds directly. Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented in part. He agreed the Act was passed under the spending power but argued that section 6010 and its legislative history establish requirements that participating States must meet, linked to the state-plan assurances in section 6063; he would hold the rights enforceable under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 per Maine v. Thiboutot, and would vacate and remand so that the lower court could follow the Rosado v. Wyman approach, giving the State a chance to comply or forgo federal funds, rather than supervising Pennhurst through a Special Master.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep451/usrep451001/usrep451001.pdf"},"1983/83-5596":{"facts":"Joseph Spaziano was indicted for first-degree murder two years and one month after the alleged offense in 1973; Florida had a two-year limitations period for noncapital offenses and none for capital ones. At the close of the evidence the trial court offered to instruct the jury on the lesser included noncapital offenses if Spaziano would waive the statute of limitations on them, and he refused after the court explained the consequences. The jury, instructed only on capital murder, convicted him of first-degree murder, and a majority at the advisory sentencing hearing recommended life imprisonment. The trial judge nonetheless imposed death, finding two aggravating circumstances (a heinous, atrocious and cruel killing and a prior violent felony) and no mitigating circumstances.","procedure":"The Florida Supreme Court affirmed the conviction, rejecting a Beck v. Alabama claim, but reversed the death sentence because the judge had relied on an undisclosed confidential part of the presentence report. On remand the trial court held a new hearing and again imposed death, and the Florida Supreme Court affirmed, finding no constitutional problem with the judge's override of the jury's life recommendation. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the trial court violate the Constitution by refusing to give a lesser included offense instruction unless the defendant waived the statute of limitations, and by overriding the jury's recommendation of life imprisonment to impose a death sentence?","holding":"No. Under Beck v. Alabama a defendant need not be forced to give up the limitations defense, and may instead choose between the instruction and the defense; Spaziano knowingly chose the defense. The Constitution does not require that a jury's life recommendation in a capital case be final or that the jury impose the sentence, so the Florida override procedure does not violate the Sixth, Eighth or Fifth Amendment, and its application here was not arbitrary.","reasoning":"Beck's concern is the distortion of the jury's fact-finding when it faces an all-or-nothing choice, and an instruction on offenses for which the defendant cannot be convicted would not improve rationality and would mislead the jury, so the defendant may choose between the instruction and the limitations defense. The Sixth Amendment has never been thought to guarantee a jury determination of the appropriate punishment, and a capital sentencing, though like a trial for double jeopardy purposes, involves the same fundamental sentencing issue. The Furman line of cases seeks measured, consistent application and individualized consideration, and nothing in those objectives requires a jury as sentencer; retribution and community expression are served by the legislature's definition of death-eligible crimes. That 30 of 37 capital jurisdictions give the decision to the jury does not make judicial sentencing offensive to contemporary standards of decency. Because a judge may be sole sentencer, jury advice is not a judgment for double jeopardy purposes: “The advice does not become a judgment simply because it comes from the jury.” As applied, the Tedder standard, the judge's written findings and mandatory Florida Supreme Court review gave no sign of arbitrariness, and the two aggravating circumstances supported the sentence.","separate":"Justice White, joined by Justice Rehnquist, concurred in part and in the judgment, joining everything except the dictum in Part II suggesting that Beck requires a state court to allow a defendant to waive limitations and receive a lesser included offense instruction on a time-barred offense. Justice Stevens, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, joined Part II but dissented from the rest. He argued that because the death penalty rests on retribution and the community's moral outrage, the Eighth Amendment, in light of history, near-universal state practice, Florida's origin of the override in an attempted response to Furman, and the greater ability of a representative jury to reflect community values, requires that the state persuade a jury that death is appropriate, so that overriding a life recommendation imposes a disproportionate punishment.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court, joined in full by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Powell and O'Connor; Justices White and Rehnquist joined all but a portion of Part II, and Justices Brennan, Marshall and Stevens joined Part II only.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep468/usrep468447/usrep468447.pdf"},"1984/84-351":{"facts":"Douglas Scanlon, who has diabetes and no sight in one eye, alleged that in 1978 Atascadero State Hospital, a California state facility, refused to hire him as a graduate student assistant recreational therapist solely because of his physical handicaps. He sued the hospital and the California Department of Mental Health in federal court under § 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, seeking compensatory, injunctive and declaratory relief. Section 505 of the Act makes the remedies of Title VI of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 available against “any recipient of Federal assistance.”","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the complaint as barred by the Eleventh Amendment. The Ninth Circuit first affirmed on a different ground, the Supreme Court vacated and remanded in light of Consolidated Rail Corp. v. Darrone, and on remand the Ninth Circuit reversed, holding that California had implicitly consented to suit by participating in and receiving funds under the Act. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict with the First and Eighth Circuits.","question":"Are States and state agencies subject to suit in federal court for retroactive monetary relief under § 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, or does the Eleventh Amendment bar such suits?","holding":"The Eleventh Amendment bars the suit. California did not waive its immunity through its constitution, the Rehabilitation Act does not clearly abrogate state immunity, and accepting federal funds under the Act does not show consent to suit in federal court.","reasoning":"Waiver and abrogation both require an unequivocal indication, because the Eleventh Amendment protects the constitutional balance between the States and the Federal Government and a State's interest covers not only whether but where it may be sued. Article III, § 5 of the California Constitution, allowing suits against the State “in such manner and in such courts as shall be directed by law,” does not specify federal court and merely authorizes the legislature to waive immunity. For abrogation, the Court held that Congress must express its intention “in unmistakable language in the statute itself,” a rule that ensures certainty and reflects that courts are deciding whether their own jurisdiction has been expanded. Section 504 and § 505 speak of remedies against “any recipient of Federal assistance,” but States are not like other recipients, and a general authorization for suit in federal court is not unequivocal enough. As to the Spending Clause theory, the Act falls far short of showing a clear intent to condition participation in its programs on a State's consent to waive immunity, so receipt of funds is not consent. The Court also defended Hans v. Louisiana and its line of cases against the dissents' calls to reexamine them.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Marshall, Blackmun and Stevens, dissented. He argued that the language and legislative history of § 504 show Congress plainly meant to bind and subject the States to suit, that the Court's clear-statement rules are hurdles designed to keep suits out of federal court rather than tools for finding congressional intent, and that the doctrine rests on mistaken history, offering a lengthy account of the ratification debates, Chisholm v. Georgia, the Eleventh Amendment's text and the Marshall Court cases to argue that neither Article III nor the Amendment embodies a general state sovereign immunity in federal-question cases. Justice Blackmun, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall and Stevens, dissented separately, joining Justice Brennan, likening the Eleventh Amendment cases to the Tenth Amendment doctrine abandoned in Garcia, and stating that he would also find that California consented by accepting funds and that Congress acted under § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment. Justice Stevens dissented separately, explaining that he joined Justice Brennan's dissent and now regarded Edelman v. Jordan and Hans as incorrectly decided, because the Court had not felt bound by stare decisis in expanding sovereign immunity.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Rehnquist and O'Connor (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep473/usrep473234/usrep473234.pdf"},"1979/79-134":{"facts":"Consolidated Edison put a bill insert in its January 1976 billing envelopes arguing that the benefits of nuclear power far outweigh any risk. The Natural Resources Defense Council asked to include a rebuttal in a later envelope, and when Con Edison refused, it asked the New York Public Service Commission to open the envelopes to contrasting views. In 1977 the Commission denied that request but prohibited utilities from using bill inserts to discuss political matters, including nuclear power. It reasoned that customers receiving bills are a captive audience who should not be subjected to the utility's views on controversial public policy issues. It still allowed inserts on other topics, such as energy conservation.","procedure":"The state trial court held the order unconstitutional, but the Appellate Division reversed and the New York Court of Appeals affirmed, treating the order as a valid time, place, and manner regulation protecting customer privacy. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Does the Commission's order prohibiting utilities from including inserts discussing controversial issues of public policy in monthly bills violate the First and Fourteenth Amendments?","holding":"Yes. The order directly infringes protected speech and is neither a valid time, place, or manner restriction, nor a permissible subject-matter regulation, nor a precisely drawn means of serving a compelling state interest.","reasoning":"The Court first held that Con Edison's status as a corporation does not deprive it of free speech protection, following Bellotti, and that the ban \"strikes at the heart of the freedom to speak\" on public issues. A time, place, or manner rule must be content-neutral, but the Commission admitted it barred inserts because of their subject matter, allowing some topics and forbidding controversial issues. Applying the ban to all views on a subject does not save it, since the First Amendment's hostility to content regulation reaches prohibitions of an entire topic. Greer v. Spock and Lehman v. Shaker Heights were narrow exceptions concerning access to government property and did not apply to a private party using its own billing envelope. Under strict scrutiny, the captive-audience rationale failed because customers can avoid the message \"simply by transferring the bill insert from envelope to wastebasket.\" The limited-space rationale failed because envelopes are not a scarce resource like broadcast frequencies and the record did not show inserts would crowd out others. The cost-subsidy rationale failed because the Commission had not shown it could not allocate costs to shareholders and did not rest its order on that ground.","separate":"Justice Marshall concurred, stressing that the decision does not address whether the Commission may exclude insert costs from the rate base. Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment, saying that the Court's statement that time, place, or manner rules may never rest on subject matter is overbroad; he would rest the decision on the point that a regulation motivated by a wish to curtail a particular point of view on controversial issues abridges speech, and that offensiveness of a message cannot justify censorship. Justice Blackmun dissented, joined by Justice Rehnquist as to Parts I and II. He argued that using the billing envelope for the utility's message is a forced subsidy by ratepayers of a state-created monopoly, that the State may prevent this, and that even cost allocation would not eliminate the free ride; he also noted ways States might still address the problem.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, and Marshall; Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment, and Justice Blackmun dissented, with Justice Rehnquist joining Parts I and II.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447530/usrep447530.pdf"},"1985/84-6859":{"facts":"Petitioner Skipper was convicted of capital murder and rape in a South Carolina court, and a separate sentencing hearing was held before the jury. The State presented evidence of his history of sexually assaultive behavior. Skipper offered mitigating testimony from himself and from his former wife, mother, sister and grandmother, and he and his former wife briefly said he behaved well during the 7 1/2 months he spent in jail awaiting trial. He also sought to call two jailers and a regular visitor to testify that he had made a good adjustment in jail, but the trial court excluded this as irrelevant under State v. Koon. In closing, the prosecutor argued that Skipper would pose disciplinary problems and would likely rape other prisoners. The jury sentenced him to death.","procedure":"The South Carolina Supreme Court affirmed the death sentence, stating that evidence of future adaptability to prison was properly refused and that evidence of past adaptability had come in through other witnesses. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari to consider whether this conflicted with Lockett v. Ohio and Eddings v. Oklahoma.","question":"Did the trial court's exclusion of testimony from jailers and a visitor about the defendant's good conduct in jail deny him the right to place relevant mitigating evidence before the capital sentencing jury?","holding":"Yes. Under Lockett and Eddings, the sentencer may not be precluded from considering any relevant mitigating evidence, and evidence that a defendant would behave well in prison if spared is potentially mitigating. Excluding the testimony was error that could not be treated as harmless, so the death sentence could not stand.","reasoning":"The Court began with the well-established rule that the sentencer may not refuse to consider \"any relevant mitigating evidence.\" Testimony that Skipper had been a well-behaved prisoner could support favorable inferences about his character and probable future conduct in prison, and so could serve as a basis for a sentence less than death; predicting future conduct is an inevitable part of sentencing, and future dangerousness may be treated as an aggravating factor. The Court rejected the State's three arguments. The record did not show the ruling was merely an application of rules against lay opinion, since counsel expected the witnesses to testify that he had made a good adjustment. The State's distinction between past conduct offered for character and future adaptability was elusive, and the ruling in fact barred the evidence for any purpose. Nor was the evidence merely cumulative, since the jury would tend to discount the self-serving testimony of Skipper and his family but give greater weight to disinterested jailers, particularly as the prosecutor stressed Skipper's dangerousness in prison. The Court did not hold that every facet of prison adjustment is relevant. It added that due process also requires an opportunity to respond to a prosecutor's reliance on future dangerousness.","separate":"Justice Powell, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, concurred in the judgment. He would reverse on the narrower ground of Gardner v. Florida, because Skipper was not allowed to rebut the prosecutor's evidence and argument about his conduct in jail. He disagreed that Lockett and Eddings reach post-arrest conduct, which does not reduce culpability for the crime, argued that States should decide what evidence is relevant, and said that States could reasonably exclude good jail behavior as having little predictive value and as undermining deterrence.","disposition":"The judgment of the South Carolina Supreme Court was reversed insofar as it affirmed the death sentence, and the case was remanded for further proceedings, with the State free to seek death again through a new sentencing hearing. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall, Blackmun, Stevens and O'Connor. Justice Powell concurred in the judgment, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep476/usrep476001/usrep476001.pdf"},"1979/78-6621":{"facts":"Beck was tried in Alabama for the capital offense of robbery in which the victim is intentionally killed by the defendant. His own testimony established that he took part in the robbery of an 80-year-old man, Roy Malone, but he consistently denied that he killed Malone or intended his death; he said his accomplice unexpectedly struck and killed the man. Under Alabama's death penalty statute, the judge was prohibited from instructing the jury on lesser included offenses, even though felony murder was a lesser included offense and the State conceded the testimony would otherwise have entitled him to that instruction.","procedure":"The jury could only convict of the capital crime, which required a death sentence, or acquit. It convicted him and fixed the death penalty, and after a hearing on aggravating and mitigating circumstances the trial judge refused to overturn the sentence. The Alabama Court of Criminal Appeals and the Alabama Supreme Court rejected his constitutional attack on the prohibition, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a death sentence constitutionally be imposed after a jury verdict of guilt of a capital offense when the jury was not permitted to consider a verdict of guilt of a lesser included noncapital offense, and the evidence would have supported such a verdict?","holding":"No. A death sentence may not constitutionally be imposed after a jury verdict of guilt of a capital offense where the jury was not permitted to consider a verdict of guilt of a lesser included noncapital offense that the evidence would support.","reasoning":"The Court explained that a lesser included offense instruction gives the jury a \"third option\" that ensures the defendant receives the full benefit of the reasonable-doubt standard; without it, a jury convinced the defendant is guilty of some serious crime but doubtful about an element of the capital offense is likely to resolve doubts in favor of conviction. Alabama's practice was unique, since federal courts, other states, and Alabama itself in noncapital cases require such instructions when evidence warrants. Because death is different, and the Court had demanded reliability in capital sentencing, the same concern applies to the guilt determination. It rejected Alabama's argument that an apparently mandatory death penalty makes juries more careful, finding that the statute \"interjects irrelevant considerations into the factfinding process\", encouraging conviction because the jury thinks the defendant deserves punishment, or acquittal because he does not deserve death, with no guiding standards. A mistrial is no adequate substitute, and the judge's later sentencing hearing cannot be presumed to correct a flawed verdict, since the judge would never reach the question if the jury had convicted only of a lesser offense and the jury's verdict tends to influence the judge.","separate":"Justice Brennan concurred, adhering to his view that the death penalty is always cruel and unusual punishment. Justice Marshall concurred in the judgment, likewise adhering to that view while agreeing the prohibition on lesser included offense instructions is unconstitutional because it increases the risk of error. Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice White, dissented, arguing the Court lacked jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. section 1257 because the Alabama Supreme Court's opinion addressed only state constitutional issues and it is assumed a federal question not passed on was not properly presented below.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Stewart, Blackmun, and Powell; Justice Marshall concurred in the judgment; Justices Rehnquist and White dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep447/usrep447625/usrep447625.pdf"},"1984/84-335":{"facts":"In 1970 the FBI learned that members of an antiwar group planned to blow up heating tunnels linking federal buildings in Washington and had discussed kidnapping the National Security Adviser. Attorney General John Mitchell authorized a warrantless wiretap on the phone of Professor William Davidon, a group member, for national security intelligence. From late November 1970 to January 1971 the tap intercepted three apparently innocuous conversations between Davidon and Keith Forsyth, who learned of it in 1972. After this Court held in Keith that the Fourth Amendment forbids warrantless wiretaps in domestic national security cases, Forsyth sued Mitchell for damages under the Fourth Amendment and Title III.","procedure":"The District Court rejected Mitchell's claims of absolute immunity and, applying Harlow v. Fitzgerald, held he was not entitled to qualified immunity because Keith merely extended settled principles; it granted Forsyth summary judgment on liability. The Third Circuit affirmed the denial of absolute immunity but held the denial of qualified immunity was not immediately appealable under the collateral order doctrine. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is the Attorney General absolutely immune from damages suits for authorizing warrantless wiretaps for national security purposes? Is a district court's denial of qualified immunity immediately appealable before final judgment? And was Mitchell entitled to qualified immunity?","holding":"The Attorney General is not absolutely immune for his national security functions. A denial of qualified immunity, to the extent it turns on a question of law, is an appealable final decision under 28 U.S.C. section 1291. Mitchell was entitled to qualified immunity because the wiretap's illegality was not clearly established in 1970.","reasoning":"On absolute immunity, the Court said Cabinet status alone does not suffice and Mitchell identified no historical or common-law basis for immunity for national security tasks. Such tasks are carried out in secret and do not generate the same vexatious litigation as judicial functions, and they lack built-in checks that correct abuses, so the risk that officials disregard rights in their zeal to protect security counsels against absolute immunity. On appealability, qualified immunity under Harlow is \"an immunity from suit rather than a mere defense to liability\" and like absolute immunity is effectively lost if the case goes to trial; denial conclusively decides the question and the appealable issue, whether the law was clearly established, is a legal one conceptually separate from the merits. On the merits, the Court reviewed the long executive practice of warrantless national security wiretaps, Katz's express reservation of the national security question, Title III's neutrality, and two district court decisions upholding such wiretaps. It concluded the question was open in 1970, and \"The decisive fact is not that Mitchell's position turned out to be incorrect\"; the District Court's reasoning that he gambled and lost was the hindsight Harlow rejected.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in part, joining Parts I, III, and IV, but would have held that the Attorney General has absolute immunity for national security acts as an aide to the President. Justice O'Connor, joined by the Chief Justice, concurred in part, joining Parts I, III, and IV and the judgment but declining to reach absolute immunity. Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment, arguing that Mitchell was acting under the President's delegated power and, since Congress declined to create a damages remedy for such wiretaps, he is entitled to absolute immunity. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, joined Parts I and II but dissented on appealability, contending a qualified immunity denial is neither completely separate from the merits nor effectively unreviewable after final judgment, and that the Court mischaracterized the record because the District Court never resolved whether the wiretap was truly for national security.","disposition":"Affirmed in part and reversed in part: the denial of absolute immunity was affirmed, and the Court of Appeals' judgment was reversed to the extent it left in place the denial of qualified immunity. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, in which Justice Blackmun joined; Chief Justice Burger and Justice O'Connor joined Parts I, III, and IV; Justices Brennan and Marshall joined Parts I and II. Justices Powell and Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep472/usrep472511/usrep472511.pdf"},"1986/85-782":{"facts":"Respondent, a Nicaraguan citizen, entered the United States in 1979 as a visitor and overstayed, and the INS began deportation proceedings. She conceded she was in the country illegally but sought withholding of deportation under section 243(h) and asylum as a refugee under section 208(a). Her evidence centered on her brother, who had been tortured and imprisoned in Nicaragua for political activity; she and he testified that the Sandinistas would interrogate her about him and learn of her own opposition to them, and she claimed she would be tortured if returned.","procedure":"The Immigration Judge applied the same standard to both claims, found no \"clear probability of persecution\" and denied both forms of relief; the Board of Immigration Appeals affirmed. The Ninth Circuit, where she pressed only the asylum claim, held that the \"well-founded fear\" standard is more generous than the clear-probability standard and remanded to the BIA, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict among the circuits.","question":"Must an alien show a clear probability (more likely than not) of persecution, the standard for mandatory withholding of deportation under section 243(h), to establish the \"well-founded fear of persecution\" that makes her a refugee eligible for discretionary asylum under section 208(a)?","holding":"No. The section 243(h) clear-probability standard does not govern asylum eligibility under section 208(a). An applicant need not prove that persecution is more likely than not in order to show a well-founded fear of persecution. The Court did not attempt to define in detail how the well-founded fear test is to be applied.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statutory text: section 243(h) (\"would be threatened\") has no subjective component and requires objective proof that persecution is more likely than not, whereas \"fear\" in the refugee definition turns partly on the applicant's state of mind. It reasoned that \"One can certainly have a well-founded fear of an event happening when there is less than a 50% chance of the occurrence taking place.\" and that Congress, which drafted section 208(a) and amended section 243(h) at the same time, kept different language in the two provisions. Legislative history confirmed this: the pre-1980 conditional-entry statute used a more lenient fear-based standard, Congress meant to conform the Act to the United Nations Protocol, whose \"well-founded fear\" definition was understood to require only good reason or a reasonable possibility of persecution, and Congress adopted the House bill rather than the Senate version that would have limited asylum to those entitled to section 243(h) relief. The Court rejected the INS argument that it is anomalous for the asylum standard to be easier, because asylum remains discretionary while withholding is mandatory. Finally, it declined to defer to the BIA, because whether the standards are identical is \"a pure question of statutory construction for the courts to decide,\" distinct from the case-by-case application of the term to facts, and the BIA's own position had been inconsistent.","separate":"Justice Blackmun concurred, stressing that the Court's opinion points the INS to the sources (the statutory language and its subjective and objective components, the Protocol and its history) from which to develop the meaning of well-founded fear, and criticizing the INS's earlier reading. Justice Scalia concurred in the judgment only: he thought the plain meaning and structure of the Act settled the matter, so the long legislative-history inquiry and the discussion of deference to the agency were unnecessary, and he believed the Court's remarks on deference under Chevron misread that decision. Justice Powell, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice White, dissented: he argued that the BIA's Matter of Acosta interpretation (a four-part test requiring a realistic likelihood of persecution) was reasonable and deserved deference, that the statutory language and legislative history are ambiguous, and that in any event the BIA in this case had applied even the more lenient standard, so the Ninth Circuit should be reversed.","disposition":"Affirmed (Ninth Circuit judgment upholding the remand to the BIA). Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall, Blackmun, and O'Connor; Justice Scalia concurred in the judgment; Justices Powell, White, and the Chief Justice dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep480/usrep480421/usrep480421.pdf"},"1980/83_orig":{"facts":"In 1978 Louisiana enacted a \"First-Use Tax\" of seven cents per thousand cubic feet on natural gas brought into the state that had not already been taxed by another State or the United States. Its main effect fell on gas from the federal Outer Continental Shelf (OCS) piped to Louisiana processing plants and mostly sold to consumers in over 30 other States. The tax was imposed on pipeline companies but was designed to be passed on to consumers, and the Act declared it a cost of preparing the gas for market and barred shifting it to anyone except the ultimate consumer. Exemptions and credits largely shielded Louisiana users of OCS gas.","procedure":"Several States, later joined by the United States, FERC and pipeline companies, sued in the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction, claiming violations of the Supremacy and Commerce Clauses. The Special Master recommended denying Louisiana's motion to dismiss but also denying the plaintiffs' motion for judgment on the pleadings pending evidentiary hearings. Exceptions to the reports were argued before the Court.","question":"Is this an appropriate case for the Court's original jurisdiction despite pending state-court suits, and, if so, does the First-Use Tax violate the Supremacy Clause or the Commerce Clause so that judgment may be entered without further hearings?","holding":"The Court accepted original jurisdiction and held that § 1303C of the Act violates the Supremacy Clause and that the First-Use Tax is unconstitutional under the Commerce Clause because it discriminates against interstate commerce. Judgment enjoining collection was ordered without further hearings.","reasoning":"The plaintiff States have standing as major gas purchasers whose costs rose as a direct result of the tax, which was clearly meant to be passed on, and also as parens patriae for citizens who cannot sue for refunds in Louisiana. The Court found the case distinguishable from Arizona v. New Mexico: the plaintiff States were not parties to the state suits, the state forum could not give interim injunctive relief, and the tax reached millions of consumers in over 30 States and implicated federal OCS interests. On the Supremacy Clause, determining pipeline and producer costs belongs to FERC in the first instance, and § 1303C, by dictating that the tax is a processing cost passed to consumers and barring reimbursement from owners of extracted hydrocarbons, usurps that authority, as in Northern Natural Gas. On the Commerce Clause, the gas flow is continuous interstate commerce, and although \"interstate commerce may constitutionally be made to pay its way\", the exemptions and credits protect Louisiana consumers and producers while the tax burdens gas leaving the state. The tax cannot be a compensatory tax because Louisiana has no sovereign interest in compensation for severance from federally owned OCS land, and exact degrees of discrimination need not be shown.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, saying there is much validity in Justice Rehnquist's dissent and the Court should stay alert to efforts to expand original jurisdiction, but he joined the opinion. Justice Rehnquist dissented, arguing the case was not appropriate for original jurisdiction: a State suing only as a consumer or on behalf of consumers asserts no sovereign interest, parens patriae standing requires sovereign or quasi-sovereign interests, and the same issues were being litigated in Louisiana state courts, so he would have dismissed. He did not reach the merits.","disposition":"Exceptions to the Special Master's reports sustained in part and overruled in part; judgment for the plaintiffs holding the tax invalid and enjoining further collection, with jurisdiction retained to implement it. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall, Blackmun and Stevens. Justice Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep451/usrep451725/usrep451725.pdf"}});